libmdnsd: make the installed headers usable outside the build tree

mdnsd.h and 1035.h are both installed (nobase_include_HEADERS), and both
open with an unconditional #include "config.h" -- a private autoconf
artifact that is never installed. Inside the build tree it resolves and
nobody notices; from a sysroot it is a hard error, so the library cannot
be linked against by anything except its own daemon.

Guard it the way autoconf intends. automake already passes -DHAVE_CONFIG_H
(DEFS in every generated Makefile here), so the in-tree build still gets
config.h and everything it sets -- _GNU_SOURCE among them -- while an
external consumer skips it and is unaffected.

1035.h then needs its netinet/in.h and sys/socket.h includes unconditional.
They were gated on HAVE_NETINET_IN_H / HAVE_SYS_SOCKET_H, which without
config.h are simply undefined, which would leave struct in_addr undeclared
for exactly the consumers this is meant to serve. Both headers are POSIX
and present on every target this builds for; the probes date from
portability to systems it no longer runs on.

Upstream: not submitted.
--- a/libmdnsd/mdnsd.h
+++ b/libmdnsd/mdnsd.h
@@ -30,7 +30,9 @@
 #ifndef LIB_MDNSD_H_
 #define LIB_MDNSD_H_
 
-#include "config.h"
+#ifdef HAVE_CONFIG_H
+# include "config.h"
+#endif
 #include "1035.h"
 #include <sys/time.h>
 #include <stdbool.h>
--- a/libmdnsd/1035.h
+++ b/libmdnsd/1035.h
@@ -37,14 +37,12 @@
 #ifndef MDNS_1035_H_
 #define MDNS_1035_H_
 
-#include "config.h"
-#include <arpa/inet.h>
-#ifdef HAVE_NETINET_IN_H
-# include <netinet/in.h>
-#endif
-#ifdef HAVE_SYS_SOCKET_H
-# include <sys/socket.h>
+#ifdef HAVE_CONFIG_H
+# include "config.h"
 #endif
+#include <arpa/inet.h>
+#include <netinet/in.h>
+#include <sys/socket.h>
 
